  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 228 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nConvoluted pattern anticipated for the upcoming holiday week though \nmajor travel impacts do not appear likely for much of the region. \nDeep troughing will continue to translate east across the Hudson Bay \nwith strong upper flow lifting north and east towards New England. A \nweaker trough will depart the Great Basin and cross the southern \nRockies through the latter half of the day on Monday. Little has \nchanged in the thinking with respect to this feature: fairly quick \nweakening with upper dynamics displayed well to the south should \npreclude any changes to the sensible weather locally. To the effect, \nNBM QPF probabilities have all but been removed from the local area \nfor late Monday into early Tuesday. Though southeasterly low level \nflow should prevail across the area, this will be offset by the \nincreasing influence of strong high pressure settling into the upper \nMississippi Valley. Towards the middle of the week, a more \npronounced shortwave will dive south across the Dakotas and enter \nnorth central and eastern Nebraska by early Wednesday. Though \nmoisture availability remains somewhat in question, have \nreintroduced limited PoPs for our north central zones in \ncoordination with neighboring offices. Though forecast soundings \nsuggest dry air at the surface will need to be overcome, given the \nbusy holiday period and greater certainty immediately to the \nnortheast, have elected to get a mention in to aid with messaging \nduring the holiday period. Any local impacts, should any occur, will \nbe fairly minor at worst. Though the northern stream remains active \nthrough the late week, increased northwesterly flow should remain in \nplace for much of the region and this will keep temperatures in \ncheck somewhat. Daytime highs in generally in the 40s can be \nexpected for Wednesday and Thursday but further increases to more \nexpansive 50s appears probable for the weekend. This should keep the \nweather quiet, outside the passage of a few frontal boundaries, for \nthe backside of the holiday travel period.",
